<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Samos</h1><p class="st-meta">1834 CE – 1912 CE · capital Vathy</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Christian principality on the island of Samos under Ottoman suzerainty, created after the Greek War of Independence and united with Greece in 1912.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/samos">Samos</a> fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-protocol-1830">London Protocol</a> of 1830 left it outside the new Greek state. As a compromise the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> made it an autonomous principality, established in 1834, with a Christian prince appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Porte</a> and paying an annual tribute. Its first prince was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefanos-vogoridis">Stefanos Vogoridis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince, usually a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariot Greek</a>, governed with an elected assembly and his own administration, flag and later postage stamps, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vathy-samos">Vathy</a>. Samians paid low taxes and enjoyed religious freedom. The economy rested on wine, especially muscat, tobacco and shipping. Politics were often turbulent, with clashes between princes and local factions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, the Samian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistoklis-sofoulis">Themistoklis Sofoulis</a> drove out the last Ottoman garrison and the assembly declared union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in November 1912. Greek troops landed in March 1913, and in 1914 the great powers confirmed its union with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-samos.jpg" alt="Principality of Samos" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princely_Flag_of_the_Principality_of_Samos_(1834%E2%80%931912).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1834 CE – 1912 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vathy</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stefanos Vogoridis, Andreas Kopasis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hegemony of Samos, Samos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1873&amp;focus=principality-of-samos" aria-label="Principality of Samos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5353 821 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Samos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5673 1077L5709 970M5673 1077L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 1077h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1873&amp;focus=principality-of-samos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
